<!-- YAM teaching handle v1: a passive two-part pistol grip (handle shell + squeeze trigger) that
     replaces a gripper on the arm flange. Geometry, frames, masses and inertias come from the
     ONShape export of the YAM + teaching-handle assembly ("YAM handle long"), whose arm chain is
     bit-identical to arm/yam/v1/yam.urdf down to link5, so its `dof_joint6` transform is exactly
     this file's flange -> handle transform.

     The root body must stay named `gripper`: combine_arm_and_gripper_xml merges it into the arm's
     terminal mount body of that name, replaying this body's pos/quat as a <frame> over the
     children and composing the <inertial> into the mount frame by hand. Only <asset>, that body,
     <equality> and <contact> survive composition; <actuator>, <keyframe> and <default> are
     dropped, so every attribute here is written inline and the trigger carries no actuator. It
     does not need one: MuJoCo's viewer drives unactuated hinges from its Joint panel, which
     writes qpos directly.

     Root rotation is Rx(+135 deg). The export seats the handle at Rz(+90 deg) Rx(-45 deg) from
     joint6's child frame, the exact intent behind its `rpy "-0.785398 0 1.5708"`, since ONShape
     truncates rpy to 6 significant digits, which would otherwise tilt the whole handle by
     2.1e-4 deg and split two quaternion components that are equal by symmetry. That frame is no
     longer the flange: the flange now reads +X right, +Y down, +Z forward, which is R_fix
     (180 deg about (1,1,0)/sqrt(2)) from the export's, and R_fix Rz(+90 deg) Rx(-45 deg)
     collapses to a single rotation about x. Translation is zero either way: the handle root
     coincides with the flange, and R_fix fixes the origin.

     `tcp_site` sits at the shell's furthest point along the approach axis (flange +Z), the same
     way every gripper's `grasp_site` sits at its fingertips: the one mesh vertex with the largest
     flange z, on the front of the butt, 208.4 mm from the flange. It is on the shell body, not
     the trigger, so squeezing does not move it. Its orientation is the shared end-effector
     convention every other gripper uses (X down, Y and Z horizontal at qpos=0), which
     test_robot_variants' grasp-site tests enforce; only the position is handle-specific.

     The trigger hinge is named `gripper`, the repo's name for a normalized gripper command, and
     shares that name with the root body (MuJoCo keeps body and joint names in separate
     namespaces). It is deliberately not named joint7: get_robot._load_joint_limits_from_xml picks
     up every joint whose name starts with "joint", and this handle has no gripper motor; the
     robot stays at 6 DOF.

     Its `axis` and `range` are the export's verbatim, chosen so the standard normalized mapping
     `q = lo + n*(hi - lo)` over a command n in [0, 1] lands the right way round for the repo's
     "0 = closed, 1 = open" convention (sim_robot._cmd_to_mj_qpos):

         n = 0  ->  q = -0.680678 rad  (-39 deg, trigger fully squeezed = CLOSED)
         n = 1  ->  q =  0 rad         (trigger at rest              = OPEN)

     The control interfaces feed n from PassiveEncoderInfo.gripper_pos, so the rendered trigger
     follows the operator's finger; without a live encoder the viewer's Joint panel drives it.

     Masses and inertias are the export's verbatim values: handle 0.252595 kg plus trigger
     0.0049991 kg, 0.257594 kg total (README.md has their provenance). This is real load on the
     flange, and MotorChainRobot hands the composed model to MuJoCoKDL, so the handle shows up in
     gravity compensation.

     Both geoms are non-colliding, matching the previous geom-less model and the gripper-base
     convention; nothing here is meant to grasp. -->
<mujoco model="yam_teaching_handle">
  <compiler angle="radian" meshdir="assets"/>

  <asset>
    <mesh name="handle" file="handle.stl"/>
    <mesh name="trigger" file="trigger.stl"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <body name="gripper" pos="0 0 0" quat="0.38268 0.92388 0 0">
      <inertial pos="0.0018575 0.02106 -0.0802638" quat="0.7059392426719 -0.057289184102795 -0.704380485114871 -0.0470729989484305" mass="0.252595" diaginertia="0.000177304388462799 0.000970897959001009 0.00105535865253619"/>
      <geom pos="-0.0106413 -0.0134314 -0.183525" quat="1 0 0 0" type="mesh" rgba="0.301961 0.427451 0.701961 1" mesh="handle" contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
      <site name="tcp_site" pos="-0.000490393 0.0231556 -0.207129" quat="0.2705980500730985 -0.6532814824381883 0.6532814824381882 0.2705980500730985" size="0.005" rgba="1 0 0 1"/>
      <body name="trigger" pos="1e-05 0.0533184 -0.0863469" quat="1 0 0 0">
        <inertial pos="-1.91766e-09 -0.000646924 -0.00511405" quat="0.527433056094875 0.470971730933418 0.470971730933418 -0.527433056094875" mass="0.0049991" diaginertia="2.23965486366579e-07 6.5772e-07 7.14059513633421e-07"/>
        <joint name="gripper" pos="0 0 0" axis="1 0 0" range="-0.680678 0"/>
        <geom pos="-0.0106913 -0.0680085 -0.0984379" quat="1 0 0 0" type="mesh" rgba="0.94902 0.94902 0.94902 1" mesh="trigger" contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
